---
// The guide: Orioni's "Eurth country setup" tutorial from IIWiki (docs/sandbox-7.wiki),
// rewritten for the web with his permission. The steps, their order and the wikitext are the
// tutorial's; the snippets come from the same code the creator uses, so they can't drift apart.
import { article, ref, wikipedia } from '@eurth/style/build';
import Page from '@eurth/style/components/Page.astro';
import { site } from '../../eurth.config.mjs';
import { GROUPS, fieldsOf } from '../wiki/fields';
import { SECTIONS, imageLink } from '../wiki/page';
import { steps } from '../wiki/setup';
import { teland } from '../wiki/teland';

const updated = '2026-11-15';
const description = 'How to add a country to Eurth on IIWiki, step by step: the country page and its infobox, the flag, the flag shortcut, the category and the lists.';
const picture = imageLink('Selunna harbour.jpg', 'The harbour of Selunna.');
const todo = Object.fromEntries(steps(teland).map((step) => [step.id, step]));
const prompt = (text: string) => text.replace(/^\(WIP\.\s*/, '').replace(/\)$/, '');
---

<Page
  site={site}
  title="How to add a country to Eurth on IIWiki"
  description={description}
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Guide', path: '/guide/' }]}
  jsonld={[article(site, { headline: 'How to add a country to Eurth on IIWiki', path: '/guide/', description, updated })]}
>
  <article class="eu-prose">
    <p class="eu-kicker">Guide</p>
    <h1>How to add a country to Eurth on IIWiki</h1>
    <p class="eu-lead">
      Five steps take a new nation from nothing to a page that sits properly in Eurth's lore: the country page, the flag, the flag shortcut, the category and the
      lists. This is the Eurth country setup tutorial, with the <a href="/">creator</a> doing the typing.
    </p>

    <h2>Before you begin</h2>
    <p>
      If wikis are new to you, read the wiki's manual of style and skim the help pages of
      <a href={ref('https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Help:Contents', site.host)}>MediaWiki</a>, the software IIWiki runs on, and of
      <a href={ref('https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Help:Contents', site.host)}>Wikimedia Commons</a>, for how images work. Ten minutes there saves an evening
      later.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then draft in your <a href="/glossary/#sandbox">sandbox</a>, a page under your own user name. While the page is a draft, leave off the footer
      <a href="/glossary/#navbox">navboxes</a> and the <a href="/glossary/#category">category</a>: both file a page where readers browse, and a draft doesn't belong
      there yet. The creator's "For my sandbox" output leaves them out; "For the live page" puts them back.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Create the country page</h2>
    <p>
      A country page opens with the <a href="/glossary/#infobox">infobox</a>, the panel on the right with the flag and the facts. Eurth uses the standard
      <span class="eu-data">Infobox country</span> <a href="/glossary/#template">template</a> that IIWiki provides. You write a list of
      <a href="/glossary/#parameter">parameters</a>, one per line, and the wiki draws the box.
    </p>
    <p>The template is long, but it comes in eight parts, and no part is compulsory:</p>
    <ul>
      {
        GROUPS.map((group) => (
          <li>
            <b>{group.label}:</b> {group.help}{' '}
            <span class="eu-muted">
              ({fieldsOf(group.id)
                .filter((f) => !f.more)
                .slice(0, 4)
                .map((f) => f.label.toLowerCase())
                .join(', ')}
              {fieldsOf(group.id).length > 4 ? ' and more' : ''})
            </span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p>
      Fill in what you know. An empty line draws no row, so a short infobox looks finished, not broken. Three habits from the tutorial are worth keeping: mottoes
      and anthems go in inverted commas, the native name goes in italics, and when the largest city is the capital you write the word
      <span class="eu-data">capital</span> instead of repeating the name. The creator does all three for you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under the infobox comes an "under construction" notice, then the opening. Its first sentence says what the country is and where: the name in bold, the
      official name, the continent, Eurth, and the neighbours on each side. Three short paragraphs follow:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Name, location, borders, rough population and size, the capital.</li>
      <li>The political system, the history in one sentence, how it leads to the present in one more, and the head of state.</li>
      <li>The economy in two sentences and international relations in one.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>After the opening, the page has seven sections, in this order. Each asks a few questions; answer the ones that matter for your nation.</p>
    <dl>
      {
        SECTIONS.map((s) => (
          <>
            <dt>{s.title}</dt>
            <dd>{prompt(s.prompt)}</dd>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p>
      Geography comes with a small gallery of five pictures to swap for your own, and Politics with a pair of portraits for the head of state and the head of
      government. The page ends with its references. The creator's page skeleton has all of it in place, with the questions under each heading as text to replace.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Add the flags</h2>
    <p>
      Once the page is saved, the infobox shows a red link where the flag should be. The link is named after your country:
      <span class="eu-data">Flag of Teland.png</span>, and likewise for the coat of arms and the map.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Click the red link. It opens the image uploader with the right file name already set.</li>
      <li>Upload a PNG or an SVG: they make the prettiest images.</li>
      <li>Before you save, add the image to your country's category, so everything about your nation stays in one place. Put this line in the image's description:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre class="eu-data eu-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="The category line for an image">{todo.flags?.wikitext}</pre>
    <p>
      To change the flag later, open the image's page and choose "Upload a new version of this file". Every page that shows the flag follows by itself. If your flag
      comes from a nation profile, the creator hands it to you under the name the infobox asks for.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Create the country-flag shortcut</h2>
    <p>Lists across the wiki show a miniature flag beside each country's name. A small template of your own makes that work for yours.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Create the page <span class="eu-data">{todo.shortcut?.page}</span>, with your country's name in place of Teland.</li>
      <li>Paste this, with your own name and flag file:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre class="eu-data eu-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="The country data template">{todo.shortcut?.wikitext}</pre>
    <ol start="3">
      <li>Press "Show preview" to test it, and "Save changes" when it looks right. If it doesn't, ask: someone on the wiki has seen the same thing before.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>From then on, this shows your flag and your country's name wherever you write it:</p>
    <pre class="eu-data eu-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="The flag shortcode">{todo.flag?.wikitext}</pre>

    <h2>4. Create the country category</h2>
    <p>Create the page <span class="eu-data">{todo.category?.page}</span> and save it with these two lines. They point the category at your country page and file it among the countries of Eurth.</p>
    <pre class="eu-data eu-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="The category page">{todo.category?.wikitext}</pre>

    <h2>5. Add the country to the lists</h2>
    <p>
      Eurth keeps lists of its nations, gathered in <span class="eu-data">{todo.lists?.page}</span>. Add your country to the common ones. Respect each list's own rules
      and keep it sorted: most are in alphabetical order.
    </p>

    <h2>House style</h2>
    <p>Two conventions keep Eurth's pages consistent, and both save you trouble.</p>
    <p><b>Pictures.</b> In the text, write every picture the same way, on the left or the right. Other sizes and options tend not to work on IIWiki.</p>
    <pre class="eu-data eu-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="An image in wikitext">{picture}</pre>
    <p>
      <b>Links.</b> Put a <a href="/glossary/#wikilink">link</a> on the first mention of a page in each section, and not again in that section. A reader who jumps
      straight to Economy still finds the link to your capital there, and nobody wades through blue text.
    </p>

    <h2>Removing a country</h2>
    <p>Nations retire. When one does, tidy up after it:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Remove the country from the common lists.</li>
      <li>Use the wiki's "What links here" page to find any links that remain.</li>
      <li>
        Change the parent of the country's category from <span class="eu-data">Countries (Eurth)</span> to <span class="eu-data">Retired countries (Eurth)</span>.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>The pages stay, as part of Eurth's history. If you don't have a nation yet, <a href="https://eurth.org">Eurth</a> has a world with room for one.</p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        "Eurth country setup" by Orioni, a tutorial on <a href={ref('https://iiwiki.com/', site.host)}>IIWiki</a>, the wiki Eurth's members write on. This guide is that
        tutorial, rewritten for the web with the author's permission.
      </li>
      <li><a href={ref('https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Help:Contents', site.host)}>Help:Contents</a>, MediaWiki: how to edit a wiki page.</li>
      <li><a href={ref('https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Help:Contents', site.host)}>Help:Contents</a>, Wikimedia Commons: how images and their pages work.</li>
      <li><a href={wikipedia('Template:Infobox country', site.host)}>Template:Infobox country</a>, Wikipedia: every parameter of the infobox, with examples.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="eu-muted">Last updated <time datetime={updated}>15 November 2026</time>.</p>
  </article>
</Page>
